// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @expected-error: E402
// @tags: modules, mount, diagnostics, keywords
//
// Pin (T1008) — when a mount path opens with a KEYWORD segment and then
// fails, the diagnostic has to say which segment was consumed.
//
// The reported case:
//
//     mount cog.resolve.{PackageName};
//     error<E402>: module `resolve` not found
//
// The author wrote two segments; the message names one. `cog` is a path
// keyword meaning "the root of the current cog", and the path-segment
// match handles it by CLEARING every segment accumulated so far. The
// path is reset, only `resolve` survives to be looked up, and that is
// the name the error reports. The parse is CORRECT: a keyword beats a
// directory name.
//
// The message is what was wrong — it reported the consequence and hid
// the cause, and the cause is precisely the thing the author cannot
// derive from their own text.
//
// It bites in the stdlib because `core/cog/` exists: `core/cog/resolve.vr`
// declares `module core.cog.resolve;`, so the directory is real and its
// short form is unreachable by construction. `mount core.cog.resolve.{X}`
// resolves; the short form never can.
//
// The same holds for `super` and `self`, which also rewrite the path
// before resolution, so the hint is emitted for all three rather than
// special-cased to the one that was reported.
//
// This is the third defect of the day whose shape is "the message
// contradicts the source it describes" — the first was a module surface
// listing 24 names while omitting one declared in the file being listed.

mount cog.resolve.{PackageName};

fn main() {
    print("ok");
}
